-- title:
--   (A.62), OA p.36 — the Bellman inequality along a feasible solution
-- statement:
--   Let $(f,B,\gamma)$ solve (13) with $f(0)=0$, and let $(W,I,E,a,S)$ be feasible for the EWF (10). For every $t\ge0$,
--   $$\mathbf E[f(W(t))]-\mathbf E[f(W(0))]\ge\gamma t-\mathbf E\Big[\sum_ib_i\int_0^t(a_i(s)-S_i)^+\,ds\Big]-c_k\mu_k\mathbf E[E(t)].$$
--   This is the verification inequality of the lower bound, valid for controls with jumps.
--
--   **Formalization Note** All terms are moved to their nonnegative side and computed in $[0,\infty]$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: the real inequality (A.62) with differences of expectations → the same inequality with each term moved to its nonnegative side, in ℝ≥0∞; reason: f ≥ 0 on ℝ₊, W ≥ 0 and E ≥ 0; a real difference of expectations would read a junk value when an expectation is infinite
--   - reading: (10f): a(·,W(·)) is 𝔽-adapted → progressive measurability, and integrable paths (aᵢ − Sᵢ)⁺ on every [0,t]; reason: the paper integrates these paths in (10a)–(10b) and applies Tonelli in (A.47), which needs both
--   - encoding: a(t,W(t,ω),ω) → one process a t ω; reason: the allocation is used only along the path
--   - generalization: Σ := Σᵢ αᵢλᵢ(1+σᵢ²)/μᵢ² (p.15) → Σ > 0 a free parameter; reason: every statement depends on Σ only through Σ > 0; the source value is one such Σ
--   - encoding: SKU set {1,…,I}, indices j, k → Fin n with n ≥ 1; bjμj/λj and ckμk as minima; reason: only the values of the minima enter the statements
--   - encoding: f ∈ C₂ with domain ℝ₊ → f : ℝ → ℝ, C² on ℝ, with (13) imposed for w ≥ 0; reason: a C² function on [0,∞) extends to ℝ, so no solution is lost
--   - generalization: 𝔽 generated by (Xᵢ) → any filtration for which X is a Brownian motion; reason: the natural filtration is one such filtration
-- source:
--   Özkan and van Houtum, Joint Inventory and Scheduling Control in a Repair Facility, SSRN 3784144 (2023), (A.62), OA p.36

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RepairSched_Model_IsBellman
import Definitions.Def_RepairSched_Model_backorderRatio
import Definitions.Def_RepairSched_Model_IsFeasible
import Definitions.Def_RepairSched_Model_expBackorder
import Definitions.Def_AtaBarjesteh23_Workload_DriftBM

open MeasureTheory

namespace RepairSched

/-- **(A.62): the Bellman inequality along a feasible solution.**
Özkan and van Houtum, *Joint Inventory and Scheduling Control in a Repair Facility*, SSRN 3784144 (2023), (A.62), OA p.36 (PDF p.76).

Let `(f, B, γ)` satisfy (13) with `f(0) = 0`, let `X` be a Brownian motion with drift `0` and
variance `Σ` for `𝔽`, and let `(W, I, E, a, S)` be feasible for the EWF (10). For every `t ≥ 0`,
`E[f(W(t))] − E[f(W(0))] ≥ γt − E[Σᵢ bᵢ ∫₀ᵗ (aᵢ(s,W(s)) − Sᵢ)⁺ ds] − ckμkE[E(t)]`.

**Formalization Note.** Every term is moved to its nonnegative side and computed in `ℝ≥0∞`, which
is legitimate because `f ≥ 0` on `ℝ₊`, `W ≥ 0` and `E ≥ 0`. A real-valued statement would read a
junk `0` for an infinite expectation. The inequality holds with jumps of `E` and `I`, as the
paper derives in (A.50) and (A.61). -/
theorem equation_A_62 {n : ℕ} [NeZero n] (M : Model n) (S : Fin n → ℝ)
    (hEm : M.emergencyRate < M.backorderRatio) (f : ℝ → ℝ) (B γ : ℝ)
    (hBell : M.IsBellman S f B γ) (hf0 : f 0 = 0) {Ω : Type*} [mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω]
    (𝔽 : Filtration ℝ mΩ) (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P] (X W I E : ℝ → Ω → ℝ)
    (a : ℝ → Ω → Fin n → ℝ)
    (hX : AtaBarjesteh23.Workload.IsDriftBM 𝔽 P X 0 (Real.sqrt M.Sigma))
    (hfeas : M.IsFeasible 𝔽 X S W I E a) (t : ℝ) (ht : 0 ≤ t) :
    ENNReal.ofReal γ * ENNReal.ofReal t + ∫⁻ ω, ENNReal.ofReal (f (W 0 ω)) ∂P ≤
      ∫⁻ ω, ENNReal.ofReal (f (W t ω)) ∂P + M.expBackorder P S a t +
        ENNReal.ofReal M.emergencyRate * ∫⁻ ω, ENNReal.ofReal (E t ω) ∂P := by sorry

end RepairSched
